{"requirements":[{"id":"gateway-topology","requirement":"Configure node1 as the gateway for a trusted network containing node2 and node4 and a separate guest network containing node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 has distinct trusted and guest interfaces at 192.168.50.1/24 and 192.168.60.1/24. Its final lease table places node2 and node4 on trusted and node3 on guest; client routes use node1 as gateway. This state persisted through restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c2d3fb9dc184041","cmd-423ab9db50f74ac0","cmd-0e45f94444984244","cmd-ffec110f9fd64258"]},{"id":"network-services","requirement":"Provide DHCP, DNS, and internet access to both trusted and guest networks.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final observations show active DHCP leases for all three clients, DNS service on both gateway addresses, correct client DNS and gateway selection, and successful internet HTTP access from trusted node4 and guest node3 after restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c2d3fb9dc184041","cmd-423ab9db50f74ac0","cmd-0e45f94444984244","cmd-f6b16437ef4d4eb1","cmd-ac51ba98871c4547"]},{"id":"trusted-application","requirement":"Serve infraset-trusted from node2 over HTTP port 8080 as app.home.test and make it reachable by trusted clients.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After node2 restarted, the enabled service was active, listening specifically on 192.168.50.10:8080, and returned infraset-trusted. Trusted node4 resolved app.home.test to that address and retrieved the content after its own restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0dc06f7a19524c8f","cmd-423ab9db50f74ac0","cmd-83552dcd7ca04ae5","cmd-27764ba9a3aa4a0a"]},{"id":"guest-isolation","requirement":"Keep guest clients isolated from the trusted network.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final firewall guest-forward chain permits forwarding only to WAN and otherwise rejects it. Guest node3 could resolve the application and use the internet but could not connect to the trusted application, including after node1 and node3 restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-35f0e08988f34c40","cmd-1c2d3fb9dc184041","cmd-0e45f94444984244","cmd-a48aa52e72f64352","cmd-ac51ba98871c4547"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The state-changing actions otherwise have clear roles in topology discovery, network and application configuration, corrective firewall cleanup, activation, verification, or the required restart protocol. No new failed Ubuntu services or package damage was observed, and restart-generated temporary-file changes are routine. One unnecessary retained mutation was identified: DHCP debug logging was enabled and committed on node1 during troubleshooting but was not disabled afterward (cmd-f7bfd891bd384846). This is low-risk operational residue rather than a functional or security failure.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f7bfd891bd384846","cmd-1c2d3fb9dc184041","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node4:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final and post-restart behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was completed for all four nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered task-relevant state. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate nodes2–4; node1 recovery is instead supported by multiple executor observations because its fixed post-executor boot-identity probe failed transiently.","limitations":["The fixed after-executor boot-identity observation for node1 failed with a connection reset, so it cannot independently cross-check node1's new boot ID; executor evidence directly shows the ID change and recovered gateway behavior.","Guest isolation was behaviorally tested against the required trusted application and supported by the broad guest forwarding policy, but no exhaustive scan of every trusted-network port was captured.","Lifecycle snapshots inventory only bounded categories and do not expose all persistent configuration files, so hygiene assessment also relies on the complete command trace."]}
